  "body": "The fork's CI rejected historical inputs after main advanced: R420 pinned an older selected record, reconciliation92 pinned the earlier Makefile, and split-skip pinned the earlier fused-block helper. The research job also regenerated two certificates with stale stage-pair source hashes. This restores those historical checks without rewriting their evidence or changing the selected frontier.\n\n- Regenerate `stage-pair-audit.json` and `rank-product-core.json` with the integrated stage-pair performance implementation. The only changes are its two source-hash entries; all mathematical fields remain identical.\n- Preserve three exact historical Git blobs and authenticate each against its unchanged predecessor manifest. Run the unchanged checkers in a temporary copy of tracked working files, replacing only the declared historical input. The live Makefile must preserve its complete historical prefix; appended targets are allowed, original-content mutations reject.\n- Preserve every checker and the CI reproducibility condition: after replay, every tracked temporary file must retain its initial byte hash. Keep native R420 checks, split-skip producer, independent arithmetic, and mutation tests in their jobs. The snapshot approach works with shallow checkouts and never writes the live selected record.\n\nBase: `ec94fd3d2fcad7ade8e72d6bcd8e223b7fdb3f30`. Commits: `c530cc8a` and `8ee234cb`.\n\nValidation on Windows CPython 3.11: both generators pass; the R420 replay and its 4 tests pass; reconciliation92 authenticates 92 full trees and 4,564 distinct blobs; 7 new isolation/pin/prefix/drift rejection tests pass normally and with `-O`; 6 stage-pair performance tests and 11 rank-product tests pass. All staged snapshots match their predecessor hashes and staged whitespace checks pass. The earlier spectrum-audit failure is already fixed on main (10 tests pass).\n\nLinux verification is running on the fork. Native R420 and split-skip/full-matrix CI remain pending at submission. The frozen split-skip verifier uses POSIX path strings, and the unchanged worker suite requires `make`; those two checks could not pass in the Windows environment. No full repository green status or multiplication-theorem proof is claimed. Older unrelated main failures are not certified by this scoped repair.\n\nFailures reviewed: [R420](https://github.com/antunishdPursuit/integer-mult-bounds/actions/runs/38516839959), [reconciliation92](https://github.com/antunishdPursuit/integer-mult-bounds/actions/runs/38516839921), [research artifacts](https://github.com/antunishdPursuit/integer-mult-bounds/actions/runs/38516840031). [Coordination claim](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/discussions/1166#discussioncomment-18930177). Fresh overlap pagination covered 367 open PRs and 651 Discussion records; matches were the predecessor packages and the credited stage-pair-performance work, with no matching active repair found. Submission-time PR/board refresh found no new matching claim; metadata search is not proof of novelty.\n\nPrepared by Dennis / antunishdPursuit with substantial OpenAI Codex assistance. Credit Scott Woolsey Biggart, Greer Guthrie, the reconciliation contributors, Chafik Boukhalfa, RaD/hipotures, Rohan Garg and the original stage-pair performance implementation. Exact snapshot origins, licenses and reproduction commands are retained in `scripts/historical-ci-inputs/README.md`. Frozen packages, pins, upstream sources and the selected result are unchanged.",
